A||N||1||Vlad||Negative wire to ground???||||5||
Z||000000||Vlad||10-05-2000||03:06 AM||||Hello all.<P>I've noticed that I'm missing a wire from negative terminal of battery to the ground. <P>So this question is just to confirm that there has to be one and I could connect it to any place on, lets say, trank(motor) lid.<P>Am I right?<P>Also, do we have any HOWTO texts for changing<BR>the clutch in manual 86 tranny?<P>Just a sweet story... Once I bought my Fiero I came to mechanic who was taking care of my wife's car. He liked it so much so bought one for himself.  86 SE with standart, 86K km. He got it very cheap since it had a blown tranny.  He gives me to use his tools for free and helps to work on my Fiero so I kinda owe him.  <P>Any information could be usefull.<P>Thanks<P>Vlad <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||10-05-2000||08:37 AM||||The negative battery cable should have a second smaller wire (maybe a 10 gauge about 12 inches long) with a ring on it attached to the terminal end.  This wire gets grounded to the battery tray support.  There is a screw for this near where the back left corner of the plastic battery heat shield attaches to the battery tray support.  If that hole is no longer there (battery tray could be rusted out) you can attach this to any screw that makes a solid ground to the frame.  <P>The main negative battery cable attaches to a stud on the front of the engine head.  You'll see a braided ground wire already attached there that goes to the engine bay lid right hinge box.<P>There is also a large ground wire attached between the left fender well (down low) inside the engine bay and the transmission case mounting stud. <P>There are several other smaller ground wires that are bundled in the wiring harness.<P>In the case of grounding, more is better than less.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
